Delvers Core adds Lowell, Liana and Kragas as additional recruits in your own Dungeon Settlers campaign. They can appear in Custom Expedition and in the guild. Their art and character data come from your installed game; the download does not include them. Core also provides a shared API for character packs such as [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/dungeonsettlers/mods/13]Delvers Frieren[/url].

The three story characters cost no more than 500 gold each in the guild. Core also helps character packs keep unique recruits from appearing more than once, restore missing fixed traits and integrate with campaign loading. If Core detects certain errors while loading a modded campaign, it blocks further saves for that game session and displays a notice. Restart the game before saving again. This does not repair damaged saves or catch every possible error.

[size=4][b]New in 0.5.0[/b][/size]

[b]Delvers menu.[/b] In the game's options, the last tab "Delvers" shows the verified game version, whether saving is allowed, Core's settings and the installed Delvers packs. During a running campaign it is locked and only available from the title menu. There you can turn Lowell, Liana and Kragas off as founders one by one, and switch Apocalypse, its pause rule and the revive scroll on or off.

[b]Apocalypse difficulty.[/b] One step above Downfall, separately for combat and management. Choose it when you create a campaign once Downfall is unlocked. It shows in purple with its own description and tooltip. For now it uses Downfall's values. In management Apocalypse, every merchant offers about 40% fewer goods: each item keeps 60% of its amount, rounded down, at least one, and prices stay the same. In combat Apocalypse, enemies attack at once, unaware enemies take no extra damage, and the pause key slows time to half speed for up to 5 seconds instead of pausing. Without Core, the campaign loads as Downfall.

[b]Revive scroll.[/b] A living character uses it next to a dead party member, who stands up again after 20 seconds. Revives per character depend on the combat difficulty: 2 on Apocalypse and Downfall, 4 on Very Hard, unlimited below. Afterwards health and energy are capped for a while. The guild merchant always sells it, dungeon merchants sometimes. Without Core it stays a return scroll.

[b]Bleeding and first aid.[/b] In combat Apocalypse, bleeding no longer stops on its own. It keeps draining in four stages until a bandage, the aid kit, the portal or a revive stops it. Bandages are sold by merchants and made at the workbench and the alchemy table.

[b]Enemy abilities.[/b] In combat Apocalypse, some enemies cause bleeding with their hits, some casters silence their target, and spore enemies put it to sleep. Damage wakes a sleeping character.

[b]Forest Bandits.[/b] In combat Apocalypse, an enemy group of its own with its own group control. In the Vile Forest you may come across their camp, where a new ability awaits, and their boss party drops special items made for this difficulty. In the Delvers menu they can also be switched on for other difficulties.

[b]Strip equipment.[/b] Right-clicking the body of a party member or a bandit offers "Strip Equipment"; the body then drops everything it wears.

[b]Equipment stays on the body.[/b] Fallen party members keep their equipped gear. Core asks before a dressed fallen member is buried.

[b]Shield band.[/b] A honeycomb band over the bars shows a character's shield relative to their health, up to 200%.

[b]Group AI (experimental).[/b] Optional target choice, spacing, dodging and bandages for characters left on auto attack; up to Hard, off by default.

Core stores its campaign data in its own field in the save, which the game skips without Core, and in one file per campaign. Logs no longer show your account name. This is an early release, so back up your saves before using it.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

Windows x64, Dungeon Settlers DS_B.0.4.29 with the verified signature of Steam build 25846389, and BepInEx 6 for Unity IL2CPP x64, tested with 6.0.0-be.788. From 0.5.0 on, Delvers is available for BepInEx only; MelonLoader is no longer supported. Core remains inactive on an unsupported game version and reports this in the log.

If you use [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/dungeonsettlers/mods/13]Delvers Frieren[/url], update it together with Core.

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]

Extract the ZIP into the Dungeon Settlers game folder. The ZIP already contains the required folders.

Both DLLs belong in BepInEx/plugins/DungeonSettlersDelvers/ and Core's images in its Core/ folder.

Moving from MelonLoader: remove the old Core DLLs from Mods/ and UserLibs/ first. The MelonLoader versions up to 0.4.1 are being archived.

[size=4][b]Testing[/b][/size]

For 0.4.1, an in-game self-test ran on DS_B.0.4.29 under both loaders, together with Frieren 0.4.1 and Extended Hotbar 1.0.2. Test campaigns with renamed keys and with missing fixed traits were loaded, saved and loaded again. A save with an unknown effect was detected: Core blocked saving, showed its notice and refused a manual save. The offline checks passed 168 Core tests per loader without build warnings or errors.

Quickload, long campaigns and every combination with other mods have not been fully checked in game. Please include your game build, loader version and a cleaned log when reporting a problem.

